# The root filesystem overlay

Whatever is in this directory is merged into the root filesystem the image is
built with: the *contents* map to that system's root, so `rootfs-core/etc/inittab`
becomes `/etc/inittab` and replaces what the packages put there. Directories are
merged; files and symlinks overwrite.

It is applied on every rootfs build - a boot-files update included - and after
everything generated from `rootfs.toml`, so a file here is the authority over
anything the build writes.

## Core and machine trees

This tree is the **shared** half of both machines' userlands: one copy of each
file, installed onto the x86_64 image and the aarch64 image alike. The
per-machine overlays (`rootfs/`, `rootfs-aarch64/`) merge *after* it, so a
machine may still replace anything here - but the place to put a file both
machines want is this one, and the only reason to put it elsewhere is a real
hardware absence (no GPU, no PCI) or a genuine psABI difference.

`etc/inittab` carries the one rendered line: `@SERIAL_GETTY@` becomes nothing
by default, or a getty on the machine's own serial node when an interactive
boot needs the login on the wire rather than on the virtual consoles. The
substitution happens in `xtask/src/rootfs.rs` (`render_inittab`).

## What is configured here rather than in rootfs.toml

| File | Is |
|------|-----|
| `etc/inittab` | what runs at boot, and how many consoles get a getty (shared: the vcmux carries each virtual console to the wire on both machines since §13.16) |
| `etc/network/interfaces` | the network: interfaces, addressing, the hook lines - virtio-net is a device on both machines |
| `etc/resolv.conf` | the resolver - QEMU's user-mode forwarder at 10.0.2.3 |
| `etc/doas.conf` | who may escalate: `wheel`, no password |

The Wayland session's pieces are `etc/profile.d/xdg-runtime.sh` (the
`XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` libwayland refuses to start without),
`etc/local.d/xdg-runtime.start` (which makes that directory at boot) and
`etc/xdg/weston/weston.ini` (the compositor's own settings). They belong with
whichever machines run a compositor - both images carry one, weston over mesa's
software GL, so both read them.

`rootfs.toml` is for the values the build has to interpolate - the hostname, the
accounts and their passwords, the package list, the mirror, the disk layout -
because those have no file to live in. Anything that is a constant belongs here,
where changing it is editing a file rather than a Rust string.

## Which services boot

The runlevels are filled by `enable_services` in `xtask/src/rootfs.rs`
(`SERVICES`), which is the one place to read or change them: a symlink per
service, which is all `rc-update add` makes. It is the stock Alpine set -
`setup-alpine`, `setup-devd`, `setup-wayland-base` - minus the entries this
kernel cannot honour, and the comment there says which and why (no module
loader, no cgroup filesystem, a single-reader evdev queue, a link the kernel
already configured).

`seatd` is the seat manager the session runs on: weston's DRM backend asks
libseat for the card and the console, and seatd is what hands them over.
`dbus` is the session bus a stock Wayland setup starts. `local` runs the
scripts in `/etc/local.d` - which is where the runtime directory a Wayland
compositor needs comes from, because seatd (unlike elogind, which needs
cgroups) does not create one.

## Adding something

Drop it in: `rootfs-core/etc/init.d/custom-service` becomes
`/etc/init.d/custom-service` on **both** images (or `rootfs/...` for one
machine's own), and the next `cargo xtask rootfs` copies it in - no reinstall,
no package list to change. To have it *started*, name it in `SERVICES` in
`xtask/src/rootfs.rs` - that table is already both machines'.

A service script has to be executable for OpenRC to start it:

```sh
#!/sbin/openrc-run

depend() {
    need localmount
    after boot
}

start() {
    ebegin "Starting custom service"
    # Your service commands here
    eend $?
}
```
